---
// Lesson 1.1a — Launch: Fork, Clone & Press Play
// The very first class: every student forks cs-curriculum-main, clones it,
// opens it in Unity, and plays the game they will spend all semester
// rebuilding. Working backward from working code.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.1a — Launch: Fork, Clone &amp; Press Play</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Today you get the game you will be building for the next four months.
    It already works — that is the point. You fork it, clone it, press
    Play, and see every system you will learn to build this semester:
    movement, coins, spikes, a turret, an enemy. Then all year you will
    take it apart and rebuild every piece as your own.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Setup Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — Fork the starter.</strong> Log into GitHub, open
        the <code>cs-curriculum-main</code> repo your teacher links in
        Canvas, and click <strong>Fork</strong> (top right). A fork is a
        copy of the repo that belongs to <em>you</em> — your commits go to
        your copy, and you can never break the original.
    </p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Fork vs. clone — say it out loud:</strong> <em>fork</em>
        copies the repo on GitHub (server to server, yours forever).
        <em>Clone</em> downloads a repo to this machine (server to laptop).
        You do both today: fork first, then clone your fork.
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — Clone your fork.</strong> On your fork's page
        click the green <strong>Code</strong> button, copy the HTTPS URL,
        then in GitHub Desktop: File → Clone Repository → URL tab → paste
        → <strong>Clone</strong>. Or if you use the terminal:
    </p>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/cs-curriculum-main.git</code></pre>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — Open it in Unity.</strong> Open Unity Hub →
        <strong>Add</strong> → pick your cloned <code>cs-curriculum-main</code>
        folder. The first open takes a while (it imports every sprite in
        the game). When it finishes, you should see three scenes in
        <code>Assets/_Scenes</code>: <strong>Start</strong>,
        <strong>Overworld</strong>, and <strong>Platformer</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — Press Play.</strong> Open the <strong>Start</strong>
        scene and hit the Play button. Then open the
        <strong>Overworld</strong> scene and press Play again — walk with
        WASD or the arrow keys, collect a coin, touch a spike, walk near the
        turret, get chased by the enemy. Watch the Console tab while you
        do it.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: The Console is your window into the game</h3>
        <p>
            While playing the Overworld, collect a coin and watch the
            Console (Window → General → Console if it is not open). Then
            touch a spike. What two different things print, and how do the
            numbers change each time you repeat the action?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tour</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>What is actually in this repo?</strong> Find each of these
        in the Project window — you will touch all of them this year:
    </p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Folder</th><th>What lives there</th><th>Your first date with it</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/_Scenes</code></td><td>Start, Overworld, Platformer — the three levels</td><td>Today</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/Scripts</code></td><td>The C# you will rewrite: PlayerController, StatManager, TurretController, EnemyController, Lever</td><td>Lesson 1.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/Prefabs</code></td><td>Ready-made game objects: Coin, Spikes, Turret, Player, projectiles, the axe, the switch</td><td>Unit 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/Artwork</code></td><td>Top-down grass and walls; cave and entrance tiles for the platformer</td><td>Unit 7</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Assets/Animations</code></td><td>The player animator with axe and shovel variants</td><td>Lesson 1.6</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        <strong>The scene split matters all year:</strong> the same
        <code>PlayerController.cs</code> runs in every scene — but when the
        scene is <em>Platformer</em> it switches to jumping physics and
        gravity. One script, two games. You will find that
        <code>if</code> statement in Unit 5 and add to it in Unit 6.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            This starter is on GitHub, public, with an open license — you
            copied it and you will build a product on it, legally. Why is
            that allowed, and what does the license obligate you to do?
            (We settle this question for real in
            <a href="../lessons/9.10a.html">9.10a</a> — but take a guess
            now.)
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Ship It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Commit — the habit that starts today.</strong> In GitHub
        Desktop (or <code>git add</code> / <code>git commit -m</code> /
        <code>git push</code>), commit with the message
        <code>"1.1a: cloned the starter"</code> and push. From now on:
        <strong>every single class ends with a commit and a push.</strong>
        Your commit history is the receipt of your whole year.
    </p>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the difference between a fork and a clone</li>
            <li>Open any scene in Unity and press Play</li>
            <li>Find the Console tab and read a <code>Debug.Log</code> line</li>
            <li>Name the three scenes and what makes the Platformer one different</li>
            <li>Commit and push to your own fork</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — the editor tour, scripts, input, and vectors</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — every API this course uses</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.1a" title="1.1a Launch: Fork, Clone &amp; Press Play — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
